; expect 31
; MAC-HYG-8's FOURTH hole: a template's free identifier naming
; something the macro's module merely IMPORTED (2026-08-16).
;
; MAC-HYG-7 rewrites a template's free identifier to `Mod$name` for
; the macro's own module, and `364-macro-definition-site.ax` pins what
; that buys. It tries exactly ONE spelling, so a name the macro's
; module imported rather than declared found no such declaration,
; stayed bare, and meant whatever the CALL SITE could see - the same
; one-macro-two-meanings failure MAC-HYG-6 closed, in the case it did
; not reach.
;
; The rule said closing this needed import EDGES, which the merge
; consumes: `resolveDeclsPhase` reads each `(import ...)` and pushes
; nothing into the merged list. It does not need them. Every merged
; declaration carries the module it came from, so "which module
; declares this name" is answerable from the list, and when exactly
; ONE module answers, that is the one the macro's module reached.
;
; Exactly one, because two is AX3014's ambiguity by another name and
; this rewrite has no more right to choose than a bare reference does.
; Ambiguity therefore leaves the name alone, which is a REFUSAL and
; not a wrong answer: `AX3014` at the invocation, carrying the
; expansion frame that names the macro.
;
; Five claims, one bit each (31 when all hold). Two are the hole and
; three are controls, and the ablation below says which is which:
;
;    1  a LOCAL of the template's free name at the invocation does not
;       capture it: 40, the imported `tenTimes`, not the local's 0.
;       This is the hole itself
;    2  the same macro with nothing shadowing it answers 40 too, where
;       this FILE's `tenTimes` answers 9. Same macro, two meanings,
;       chosen by the caller - the failure MAC-HYG-6 closed for a
;       macro's own module, reappearing for its imports
;    4  a name the macro's module declares AND imports resolves to its
;       OWN: 6 through `LibImp$both`, where `HelpImp$both` gives 5.
;       A control - the one spelling MAC-HYG-7 already tried answers
;       it - but it is what proves the search cannot overrule the
;       module the macro is written in. Ablated (drop `LibImp`'s own
;       `both`) this file answers 27, so the search does answer it
;       when nothing else does
;    8  this file's own reference to its own `tenTimes` still means
;       this file's: 9. A control, in the direction a rewrite of bare
;       names would break first
;   16  the search reaches two edges - `deepFn` lives in `DeepImp`,
;       which `MidImp` imports and `LibImp` does not - so what it
;       walks is the merged list and not a direct-import list. This
;       file declares its own `deepFn` (answering 2) precisely so the
;       claim can fail
;
; Two ablations, both measured. The compiler before this commit
; REFUSES this file: `AX3032` at the shadowed invocation, the capture
; refusal that caught this hole without resolving it - a program that
; is correct being rejected, which is why closing the hole and not
; only reporting it was worth a commit. With that one term removed so
; the refusal cannot mask the rest, it answers 13 against 31: bits 2
; and 16 fail, each to this file's own declaration of the name.
(import LibImp)

;; This file's own `tenTimes`, which is not the macro's.
(:: tenTimes (-> Int Int))

(fn (tenTimes n) (+ n 5))

;; and its own `deepFn`, so `useDeep` has something to be captured BY
(:: deepFn (-> Int Int))

(fn (deepFn n) (- n 1))

(:: b2i (-> Bool Int))

(fn (b2i x)
  (if x
    1
    0
  )
)

;; the hole: a local of the template's free name at the invocation
(:: shadowed Int)

(fn (shadowed)
  (let ((tenTimes 
    (lambda (y) 0)
  ))
    (useImported 4)
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (one (== shadowed 40))
    (two (== (useImported 4) 40))
    (four (== (useBoth 4) 6))
    (eight (== (tenTimes 4) 9))
    (sixteen (== (useDeep 3) 300))
  )
    (+ (b2i one) (+ (* 2 (b2i two)) (+ (* 4 (b2i four)) (+ (* 8 (b2i eight)) (* 16 (b2i sixteen))))))
  )
)
